The program reads up to 100 ints from stdin, computes [38;5;10mx*x + x[0m in place, and prints each on its own line. Three things drove the translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mSelf-aliased call.[22m [38;5;10mdriver[0m calls [38;5;10mfma_array(out, out, out, out, len)[0m — all four pointers are the same buffer, which safe Rust can't express as separate [38;5;10m&mut[0m/[38;5;10m&[0m params. Since element [38;5;10mi[0m depends only on index [38;5;10mi[0m, I translated it to an in-place [38;5;10mfma_array_aliased(&mut [i32], usize)[0m, which is semantically identical rather than merely close.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mSigned overflow.[22m [38;5;10mmul1[i] * mul2[i] + add[i][0m overflows for inputs beyond ~46340 and is UB in C. gcc -O2 wraps two's-complement, so I used [38;5;10mwrapping_mul[0m/[38;5;10mwrapping_add[0m. Rust's default [38;5;10m*[0m/[38;5;10m+[0m would have panicked in debug and silently wrapped in release; being explicit makes both profiles match the C.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m[38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m semantics.[22m [38;5;10mread_line[0m-based parsing would be wrong here, so I wrote a byte-level [38;5;10mScanner[0m that reproduces the directive exactly: skips C-locale whitespace [3mincluding newlines[23m (so [38;5;10m1\n2 3[0m and [38;5;10m1 2 3[0m are equivalent), accepts an optional sign, then requires at least one decimal digit. It distinguishes matching failure (returns 0, e.g. [38;5;10mabc[0m or a bare [38;5;10m-[0m) from input failure (returns EOF), though the C treats both as [38;5;10m!= 1[0m and breaks. Out-of-range values follow glibc, which parses into a saturating [38;5;10mlong[0m and then narrows to [38;5;10mint[0m — that's why [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m prints as the result for [38;5;10m-1[0m, not a clamp to [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m. Verified against the real binary.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two notes on things I deliberately did not change, per the no-bug-fixing requirement: on a malformed token the C stops reading and processes only the values collected so far rather than erroring, and [38;5;10mint data[100][0m is uninitialized. The latter is safe because only the first [38;5;10mi[0m entries are ever read and each was written by a successful [38;5;10mscanf[0m; I used [38;5;10m[0i32; 100][0m since Rust requires initialization, with no observable difference.[0m[0m
